PLATFORM NOTES
Monday, March 2, 2026

Why platform take rates are falling

Thirty percent was the number for fifteen years. App stores, marketplaces, payment platforms: the platform took roughly a third, and the sellers grumbled and paid. In the last two years the number has been sliding everywhere at once, to 15%, to 12%, in a few cases to a flat fee. The regulators get the credit in the press coverage. I think they are a minority of the story.

The larger cause is that the platforms' leverage came from distribution, and distribution is fragmenting. When a developer could only reach phone users through one store, the store could charge what it liked. When a seller can reach buyers through a dozen channels, some of them owned by AI assistants that do not care which store the app came from, the store is a channel among channels, and channels get paid like channels: single digits.

The second cause is that the sellers got better at the parts the platform used to do for them. Payments are a commodity. Trust is handled by reviews that live everywhere. Discovery is search, and search is being unbundled from the platforms by the assistants. What is left for the platform to charge for is hosting and compliance, which are worth something, but not a third.

For companies that sell through a platform, the implication is to negotiate now, while the take rate is falling and the platform is nervous. For companies that are a platform, or hope to be, it is to stop modeling on 30%. The businesses being built now on marketplace economics will have to make money at 10%, and that is a different business.

